00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520 align:start position:0%
 
okay let's get right into audience  questions for the week first one is hey  David we've seen all sorts of different  takes and plans from Donald Trump to  governor's about how and when states  should start to reopen after the virus  what should a reasonable reopening  America plan look like so a couple  things on this number one I don't have  any independent knowledge in the sense  that I believe we should trust medical  experts and public health experts on  this and so I'm going by what those  individuals are saying not by what  politicians are saying I think there's  one other element to this that's  important in an ideal world we would  have done sort of like a full shutdown  probably around February 20 it could  have gone to four months and it would  have been over this week and we would be  in much better shape right now hindsight  is 20/20 but the point is there's a  limited political willingness to  continue expending political capital as  the protests mount and the demands mount  as crazy as the demands of the  protesters are as irrelevant as those  demands are to public health issues the  reality is the pressure is going to grow  and it's not going to be possible to  open nothing so I'm starting from the  point of not what is or would have been  the safest but what makes sense given  that the pressure to reopen at least  parts of society it will only continue  to grow number one is testing now I'm  not going to get into the full back and  forth about how Donald Trump initially  took took credit for testing even though  the testing was terrible he would insist  that it's all him now that it's clear  the testing is not up to par to reopen  Trump is saying governor's have to  figure out this testing thing let's  ignore the shirking of responsibilities  for a moment we don't have enough  testing we need at a bare minimum  500,000 tests a day most roadmaps say we  need more including like a million tests  today remember that with a million tests  a day still takes you almost a year to  test everybody one roadmap says we need  22 million tests a day so you can test  everybody on a rolling 14 day roughly  basis certainly  500 or 600 thousand tests a day must be  in place but not only that you need  proper contact tracing infrastructure in  Massachusetts here they've been hiring  work from home contact tracers different  health systems have been doing it I  think it pays around $25 an hour work  from home all states need to be hiring  proper contact tracers so that you  increase testing as you test a lot of  people as soon as you get a positive  test contact tracer comes in and lets  individuals that may have been in  contact with that infected person know  so that they can quarantine then all the  stuff about phases I'm not gonna go  piece by piece through Donald Trump's  proposal because I've already done it  but a few thoughts on that number one  there won't be a light switch that's  flipped and we are back on some of the  earlier things that should happen  include what retail elements can start  to be opened up somewhat so that they  can recover some of their revenue  remember we've got the Paycheck  protection plan but the money ran out so  there's a ton of restaurants and other  retail facing businesses that risk going  completely out of business what can be  done in restaurants in terms of can  people sit at tables out back very much  spaced out so that they feel like  they've had a dining experience but the  bag of food is placed on the table as if  it were takeout there's no interaction  with the waitstaff would that make  people feel as though we're moving in  the right direction even though in  practice it wouldn't change that much  about the risk exposure I don't know I'm  just sort of thinking out loud here  schools this semester should not reopen  it just doesn't make any sense  we should focus on shooting for the fall  but being prepared to do the fall online  people who can work from home and who  work office jobs where a lot of the work  is email and phones should not go back  to work anytime soon we should keep as  many people home as is possible to lower  the risk of loosening up some of the  public elements gyms  probably can't open right away I know  that that was included in phase one of  trumps plan does not seem reasonable  seems like just a an absolute cesspool  of germs and even if you maintain social  distancing and  I don't see how you you avoid gyms  becoming a center of spread while we  have cases and we probably can't really  loosen travel anytime soon either so for  me the the immediate loosening x' would  be we start to let people get back to  being a little bit social ideally what  we're talking about is you know a couple  that's been self isolating and another  couple that's been self isolating get  together in a house and everybody you  don't shake hands but you can hang out a  couple of hours that probably would be  like a next step as cases go down again  some of these things of you know maybe  restaurants could figure out a way if  they're large enough you reduce the  capacity by 80 percent and you let  people without table service just sit  and have their takeout in the restaurant  these are the types of things that that  need to happen and then we want to start  opening up some other retail type  businesses that may have been closed but  again remember many of those have been  open a lot of stuff hasn't actually been  closed it's just been diminished and  people are having to you know wear masks  when they are inside but those are some  of the first steps I see but really the  steps that need to happen before  anything is actually opened is testing  that needs to increase dramatically and  contact tracing needs to increase them  dramatically until we have that opening  is a real risk

